        "title": "Nutrition - Overview",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Nutrition is **applied biochemistry at the organism level** — how ingested molecules are digested, absorbed, transported, metabolized, and used or stored. The field sits at the intersection of biochemistry, physiology, epidemiology, and public health. Its central challenge: humans are complex adaptive systems, not controlled reactors, and most evidence is observational."
    },
    {
      "id": "00-OVERVIEW.md#003",
      "path": [
        "00-OVERVIEW.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:nutrition:00-overview, git-history:nutrition:00-overview]",
        "canonical_path": "nutrition/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "concepts": "[overview]",
        "current_path": "nutrition/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "id": "maxim:nutrition:overview",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "nutrition",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[overview]",
        "section": "nutrition",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Nutrition - Overview",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\nFOOD (complex mixture of macronutrients + micronutrients + phytonutrients + water)\n     │\n     ▼\nDIGESTION & ABSORPTION\n     │  Mouth: salivary amylase (starch → maltose), mastication (surface area)\n     │  Stomach: HCl (pH 1.5–3.5) + pepsin (protein denaturation + cleavage)\n     │  Small intestine: pancreatic lipase / amylase / proteases\n     │                   bile salts (emulsification of fats)\n     │                   brush border enzymes (maltase, lactase, sucrase)\n     │  → absorbed: monosaccharides, amino acids, fatty acids, glycerol,\n     │               monoacylglycerols, vitamins, minerals, water\n     ▼\nROUTING BY SOLUBILITY\n     │  Water-soluble nutrients (sugars, AAs, water-soluble vitamins):\n     │    enterocytes → portal vein → liver (first-pass metabolism)\n     │  Fat-soluble (FAs, fat-soluble vitamins A/D/E/K):\n     │    reassembled as chylomicrons → lymphatics → thoracic duct →\n     │    subclavian vein → systemic circulation (bypasses liver)\n     ▼\nLIVER: CENTRAL METABOLIC HUB\n     │  Glycolysis / gluconeogenesis / glycogenesis\n     │  β-oxidation / lipogenesis / VLDL/LDL assembly\n     │  Amino acid catabolism / transamination / urea cycle\n     │  Ketone body synthesis during prolonged fasting\n     ▼\nSYSTEMIC DISTRIBUTION & UTILIZATION\n     ├── Glucose → brain (obligate), RBCs (anaerobic), muscle (working)\n     ├── Fatty acids → heart + resting muscle (preferred fuel)\n     ├── Ketone bodies → brain (fasting), heart (efficient substrate)\n     └── Amino acids → muscle protein synthesis, gluconeogenesis, NT synthesis\n     ▼\nSTORAGE OR EXCRETION\n     ├── Glycogen: liver (~100g), muscle (~400g) — rapid mobilization\n     ├── Triglyceride: adipose tissue — bulk energy store\n     ├── Protein: no dedicated store; catabolize muscle in prolonged starvation\n     └── Excess water-soluble vitamins/minerals → urine\n```"
    },
